.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH dotman-add 1  "add "
.SH NAME
dotman\-add \- Start managing files or directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdotman add\fR [\fB\-\-for\fR] [\fB\-\-encrypt\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-plain\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-system\fR] [\fB\-\-package\-owned\fR] [\fB\-\-add\-root\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-pager\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-root\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIPATHS\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Start managing files or directories
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-for\fR \fI<CONDITIONS>\fR
Add the files as variants that apply only where these `+`\-joined conditions hold, such as `host=laptop+os!=macos`
.TP
\fB\-\-encrypt\fR
Store the files encrypted to the `[age] recipients` in .dotman.toml
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-plain\fR
Add files in plain text although their names match `[guard] secret_names` and the remote is public
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Do not ask before adding directories
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be added and change nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-system\fR
Add regular files outside $HOME that you can read, such as /etc/keyd/default.conf
.TP
\fB\-\-package\-owned\fR
With \-\-system: the file belongs to a package; manage it anyway
.TP
\fB\-\-add\-root\fR
With \-\-system: add the top\-level directory to `[system] roots` without asking
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIPATHS\fR>
Files, symlinks or directories under $HOME; with \-\-system, files outside it
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Show verbose output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress informational messages
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-pager\fR
Disable pager output
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-root\fR
Allow running as root
